The standard approach in .NET is to **wrap file system calls behind an interface** and inject it:

```csharp
public interface IFileSystem
{
    string ReadAllText(string path);
    void WriteAllText(string path, string contents);
    bool DirectoryExists(string path);
}
```

Then inject `IFileSystem` into your classes. In production, the implementation delegates to `System.IO.File`/`Directory`. In tests, you mock or stub it.

**Alternatives:**

1. **`System.IO.Abstractions`** — popular NuGet package that provides `IFileSystem` out of the box, plus an in-memory test helper (`MockFileSystem`).
2. **Microsoft's `IFileInfo`/`IDirectoryInfo`** via `Microsoft.Extensions.FileProviders` — useful if you only need reads.

**Recommendation:** Use [`System.IO.Abstractions`](https://www.nuget.org/packages/System.IO.Abstractions) unless you want to minimize dependencies. It covers the full `File`, `Directory`, `Path` surface and its `MockFileSystem` makes tests trivial:

```csharp
var fs = new MockFileSystem();
fs.AddFile("/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{}"));

var sut = new MyService(fs);
sut.DoWork();

Assert.Equal("expected", fs.File.ReadAllText("/data/output.txt"));
```
